      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">Why extracting tables from a PDF is harder than it looks</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">A PDF does not contain a table. It contains a set of text fragments, each with coordinates on a page, and the appearance of a table is produced by placing those fragments in alignment and sometimes drawing lines around them. Nothing in the file says these three fragments are one row. Extraction therefore has to reconstruct the structure: group text into rows by vertical position, infer column boundaries from where runs consistently start and end, and detect merged cells where a run spans several inferred columns. This works well when the original really was a structured table and less well when the visual alignment was loose. It is why a bank statement usually converts cleanly and a designed annual-report page sometimes does not.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">Getting a clean spreadsheet out of a financial PDF</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">A few habits improve the result. Convert page ranges containing the table rather than the whole document, so headers, footers and narrative text do not get mixed into the sheet. If the table runs across pages, expect to rejoin blocks afterwards, because a repeated header row is indistinguishable from data at the point of extraction. Check the numeric columns first — thousands separators, currency symbols and bracketed negatives are the values most likely to need reformatting once in Excel. And if the PDF is a scan, run OCR first and then verify the digits, because a misread figure in a financial table is far more damaging than a misread word in prose.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
