          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Will the table structure be preserved?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Ruled tables with clear borders and consistent column alignment convert well — headers, columns and rows come across intact, including merged cells. Tables held together only by whitespace are harder, because a PDF has no concept of a table; it just has text at coordinates. Column detection is inferred from those coordinates, so unusual layouts may need tidying after export.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Can I convert a scanned PDF to Excel?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Not directly. A scan contains an image of a table, not text, so there is nothing to extract. Run the OCR tool first to build a text layer, then convert. Accuracy on scanned financial tables depends heavily on scan quality — check the numbers carefully.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Why is my PDF table split across several sheets or rows?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Usually because the table spans multiple PDF pages. Each page is processed in turn, so a table broken by a page break, a repeated header row, or a footer in between can come through as separate blocks. Rejoin them in the spreadsheet after export.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Is this better than copying and pasting from the PDF?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Considerably. Copy-paste from a PDF flattens everything into a single column or dumps text separated by spaces, because the clipboard carries no column information. Extraction uses the x-coordinates of each text run to reconstruct where the columns actually were.</p>
        </div>
